# Answer 16 · A Critical Gender Analysis of Development Theories (CSS 2024)
@py question 2024-5

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Set out a method, analyse each of the four syllabus theories by that method, identify their shared blind spots, show the feminist responses, and apply to one Pakistani woman.
@ask Give a CRITICAL GENDER ANALYSIS of DEVELOPMENT THEORIES
(1) Introduction: every theory of development carries assumptions about women and men
(2) Method: five questions — cause, place of women, assumptions, silences, policies
(3) Modernisation: tradition blamed; women to be freed by modernity; Boserup's critique
(4) Dependency: exploitation by the centre; women absent; local patriarchy ignored
(5) World-system: core and periphery; later feminist work on households and the assembly line
(6) Structural functionalism: instrumental and expressive roles; inequality justified
(7) Shared blind spots: gender blindness, unpaid work, the harmonious household, passive women
(8) Feminist responses: WID, WAD and GAD
(9) Later critiques: capabilities, post-development, feminist economics, ecofeminism
(10) Pakistan: one cotton picker seen through each theory
(11) Conclusion: each theory holds part of the truth; gender analysis completes them
:::

## Decoding the question
**"Give a critical gender analysis of development theories"** asks you to examine the **main theories** — the syllabus names **modernisation, dependency, world-system and structural functionalism** — asking what each says and **assumes about women and men**, what it **leaves out**, and what its policies **do to gender relations**. "Critical" means judging strengths and weaknesses. A consistent **method** applied to each theory, a **comparison**, and an example make the answer analytical rather than descriptive.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
A **development theory** explains why some societies are rich and others poor, and how the poor can develop. A **critical gender analysis** asks what each theory assumes about **women and men**, what it hides, and whose interests it serves.

#### ii) The argument
All four classic theories began **gender-blind**. Each captures part of the truth — culture, global structures, institutions — but none saw **women's unpaid work**, **power inside households** or **women's agency**. Feminist scholarship exposed these blind spots and produced new approaches.

### 2. The method
Each theory is analysed by five questions: (1) what **causes** poverty; (2) where **women** appear; (3) what it **assumes** about family and work; (4) what it is **silent** about; and (5) what its **policies** do to gender relations.

### 3. Modernisation theory

#### i) The theory
**W. W. Rostow** (*The Stages of Economic Growth*, ==1960==) argued that societies pass from **traditional** to **high mass consumption** through five stages. Poverty is caused by **internal tradition**; the cure is industry, cities, education and Western values.

#### ii) Gender analysis
Women appear as **mothers** and victims of tradition, to be freed by modernity; benefits "**trickle down**" through the male breadwinner. **Ester Boserup** (==1970==) showed the opposite often happened: modernisation gave land, machines and credit to men, and **marginalised** women farmers. The theory blames culture and ignores structures, and holds up the West — which had its own patriarchy — as the model of equality.

### 4. Dependency theory

#### i) The theory
**Prebisch**, **Frank** ("the development of underdevelopment", ==1966==) and **Rodney** argued that poverty is **produced** by colonialism and unequal trade between centre and periphery.

#### ii) Gender analysis
It moves attention from culture to **global structures**, and helps explain why poor women do the **cheapest** work and why debt leads to cuts in the services women rely on. But it is **gender-blind** — it speaks of nations and classes — and it ignores **local patriarchy** and the **unpaid subsistence work** of women that subsidises cheap exports (**Maria Mies**, ==1986==).

### 5. World-system theory

#### i) The theory
**Immanuel Wallerstein** (==1974==) described one capitalist world-economy divided into **core, semi-periphery and periphery**.

#### ii) Gender analysis
At first it too ignored gender. Feminist extensions showed that households combine wages with **unpaid work**, allowing employers to pay below a living wage (**Smith and Wallerstein**, ==1992==), and that global firms rely on a **global assembly line** of young women (**Fuentes and Ehrenreich**, ==1983==). Its strength is to show that women's cheap and unpaid labour is not marginal but **central** to global capitalism. Its weakness is to portray women as **victims** of a global machine, overlooking their organising and the ways paid work can increase their bargaining power (**Naila Kabeer**, ==2000==).

### 6. Structural functionalism

#### i) The theory
**Talcott Parsons and Robert Bales** (==1955==) argued that the family is stable when the husband plays the **instrumental** role (breadwinner) and the wife the **expressive** role (carer).

#### ii) Gender analysis
It presents the gender division of labour as **functional** and natural, and so **justifies inequality**. It ignores **power, conflict** and women's **economic work**, and reflects 1950s middle-class America. It shaped development planning that treated men as producers and women as homemakers — for example, extension services that trained men as farmers and offered women only cooking classes. Its concept of **socialisation** remains useful for explaining how gender roles are learnt.

::: table | The four theories — a critical gender analysis.
@widths 2.6,3,3,2.8,2.6
Theory | Cause of poverty | View of women | Main gender critique | What it still offers
Modernisation (1960) | Internal tradition | Freed by modernity; mothers | Blames culture; men captured benefits | Education and media widen choices
Dependency (1966) | External exploitation | Absent | Gender-blind; ignores local patriarchy | Explains cheap female labour
World-system (1974) | Unequal world economy | Absent, then assembly line | Women as victims | Gendered globalisation
Functionalism (1955) | Incomplete modernisation | Expressive homemakers | Justifies inequality | Institutions, socialisation
:::

### 7. Shared blind spots
1. **Gender blindness** — "society", "the economy" and "the nation" treated as gender-neutral.
2. **Unpaid work invisible** — none counted care and subsistence work (**Marilyn Waring**, ==1988==).
3. **The harmonious household** — all assumed income is shared fairly; **Amartya Sen**'s "cooperative conflict" (==1990==) and **Bina Agarwal**'s bargaining model show households are sites of **bargaining**.
4. **Women as passive** — victims or homemakers, rarely actors.
5. **Male-centred progress** — measured by industry, cash crops and formal jobs.

### 8. The feminist responses
The critique of these theories produced three approaches. **WID** (1970s) answered modernisation by **integrating** women into development; **WAD** (late 1970s), drawing on dependency and Marxism, argued women were integrated on **exploitative** terms; **GAD** (1980s) focused on transforming **gender relations of power**, with tools such as practical and strategic needs and **empowerment** (**Kabeer**, ==1999==).

### 9. Later critiques
**Capabilities** thinking (**Sen**; **Martha Nussbaum**, ==2000==) judges development by people's real freedoms; **post-development and decolonial** writers (**Escobar**, ==1995==; **Mohanty**, ==1984==) question development itself; **feminist economics** counts care; **ecofeminism** and **feminist environmentalism** (**Agarwal**, ==1992==) link gender and nature.

### 10. Pakistan: one cotton picker
A woman picking cotton in southern Punjab illustrates the theories. **Modernisation** would blame her lack of education and traditional norms. **Functionalism** would call her a homemaker who "helps" in the fields. **Dependency** would point to low world cotton prices. **World-system** analysis would place her at the bottom of a global textile chain. A **gender analysis** adds what all of them miss: she does most unpaid care, owns no land, may not control her wages, and has no labour protection — and that she may be organising with other women. With female labour-force participation at ==24.4%==, her case is typical, not exceptional.

### 11. Conclusion
Each development theory captures part of the truth: **culture** matters (modernisation), **institutions** shape roles (functionalism), and **global structures** exploit poor countries (dependency and world-system). But all began blind to **gender relations, unpaid work and women's agency**. A critical gender analysis does not discard these theories; it **completes** them — joining the local and the global, the cultural and the structural, and asking always who works, who owns and who decides.

## Why this answer scores
- A clear **five-question method** applied consistently to all four theories.
- A **comparison table** and a list of **shared blind spots** make the analysis critical.
- The **feminist responses** and **later critiques** show the field's development.
- A single **Pakistani case** seen through each theory makes the analysis concrete.
